{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 2,
  "simulation_id": "sim-03",
  "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B018",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B027",
    "B002"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "I change my preferred configuration from B027 to B018. The quantum spin Hall case remains an outstanding discovery of a new form of electronic matter, but its proposed three-person allocation still needs a more decisive priority record, particularly concerning the experimental representative and the boundary between the quantum spin Hall effect and the broader topological-insulator field. B018 has the more securely bounded achievement at present: magic-wavelength confinement made optical-frequency precision measurement possible without the dominant trapping shift, and this principle has been realized reproducibly in independent laboratories. Katori's central architectural idea and Ye's defining experimental advances give a concise, complementary attribution. The clocks already constitute a mature physical capability for metrology, gravitation, and fundamental tests; formal redefinition of the time standard would be an institutional consequence, not a prerequisite for recognizing the discovery.",
    "case_against": "The attribution boundary for B018 requires archival scrutiny because other contributors were important in optical-frequency metrology and clock development. The citation must therefore be limited to the lattice-clock invention and development, rather than crediting the pair for all optical-clock progress or future standard redefinition. B027 remains scientifically more radical in its revelation of topological quantum matter, and could again become preferable if a narrow citation and the proposed theory-experiment allocation are shown to be historically unambiguous.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll emphasizes that the same identifiable lattice-clock principle has been independently reproduced, with a clear division between Katori's magic-wavelength concept and Ye's experimental advances.",
        "response": "I find this argument decisive in reassessing maturity. Independent realization matters not simply as a count of instruments, but because it verifies that the physical mechanism is robust across laboratories. It also makes the two-person configuration more defensible than my earlier B027 preference, whose experimental credit boundary remains less settled."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson argues that lattice clocks combine a sharp physical innovation, mature realization, and constrained attribution, while formal time-standard replacement is not necessary for recognition.",
        "response": "I agree. From the standpoint of quantitative condensed-matter and measurement physics, the crucial test is whether a well-defined principle has yielded reproducible new physical performance. B018 meets that test already. I would retain Johansson's restraint by avoiding a citation that converts anticipated metrological standardization into evidence of present merit."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson supports the quantum cascade laser as a mature, bounded device invention but questions whether Cho's heterostructure contribution belongs in the same three-person attribution.",
        "response": "That concern reinforces the general lesson that technological importance alone does not settle individual credit. B015 is a strong alternative, but the unresolved relation between enabling heterostructures and the specific cascade-laser invention is material. B018 presently offers the narrower and better matched link between its named physical principle, experimental realization, and proposed laureates."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether archival priority review confirms that Katori and Ye alone appropriately delimit the lattice-clock architecture and its defining experimental development.",
      "Whether a still narrower formulation is needed to distinguish optical lattice clocks from the broader development of optical-frequency metrology.",
      "Whether a clarified historical record for B027 would support Kane, Mele, and Molenkamp as a sufficiently complete and bounded theory-experiment configuration."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No direct personal, institutional, or collaborative connection to the proposed laureates is apparent from the supplied profile; no conflict requiring recusal is identified."
  }
}
